.\" Man page for wifi-hotspot-gui.  View with:  man ./man/wifi-hotspot-gui.1
.TH WIFI-HOTSPOT-GUI 1 "October 2026" "wifi-hotspot 1.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wifi-hotspot-gui \- graphical control panel for the Wi-Fi hotspot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wifi-hotspot-gui
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wifi-hotspot-gui
is a GTK4/libadwaita app for controlling
.BR wifi-hotspot (8).
It can also be opened as
.B "Wi-Fi Hotspot"
from the application menu.
.PP
From the app you can:
.IP \(bu 2
turn the hotspot on and off;
.IP \(bu 2
change the network name, password, security mode, maximum number of
devices and overall download limit;
.IP \(bu 2
show a QR code that phones can scan to connect (needs
.BR python3-qrcode );
.IP \(bu 2
see each connected device's data usage and live speed;
.IP \(bu 2
limit a device's speed, block it, or add it to the approved list;
.IP \(bu 2
turn on device isolation, approved-only mode, an idle auto-off timer, a
data cap, and join/leave notifications (needs
.BR libnotify-bin ).
.PP
The app reads live status from
.I /run/hotspot-status
without root.
Turning the hotspot on or off uses the passwordless
.BR sudo (8)
rule set up during installation.
Changing settings, blocking devices or showing the password goes through
.BR pkexec (1),
so polkit asks for your password.
.SH OPTIONS
None.
Standard GTK options are accepted.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/applications/local.sudar.Hotspot.desktop
The application menu entry.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/local.sudar.hotspot.policy
The polkit action used for privileged changes.
.TP
.I /run/hotspot-status
Live statistics written by the backend.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Needs a graphical session (Wayland or X11) and Python 3 with PyGObject,
GTK 4 and libadwaita 1.
.SH NOTES
On GNOME, a Quick Settings toggle is also installed as a GNOME Shell
extension.
On other desktops such as Cinnamon, MATE, XFCE or KDE, use this app or the
.BR wifi-hotspot (8)
command instead.
